  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 1209 PM MST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nCool and tranquil conditions return Sunday as an upper level \nshortwave ridge moves across the desert SW Sunday into Monday. The \nbackdoor front moving south across eastern Sunday will result in low \nclouds across far eastern NM Sunday before spreading to as far west \nas the east slopes of the central mountain chain Sunday night into \nMonday morning. Low clouds/patchy fog across eastern NM will \ngradually burn off from northwest to southeast late Monday morning \ninto early Monday afternoon before expanding back to the northwest \nagain early Tuesday morning and then burning off late Tuesday \nmorning.\n\nIncreasing clouds and south to southwest winds during the day \nTuesday as the next Pacific storm system moves east over the Great \nBasin. Guidance is in strong agreement of tracking the base of this \nupper level trough across the Four Corners region thus favoring any \nshower activity across western NM late Tuesday into early Wednesday \nand north central NM Tuesday evening into Wednesday afternoon before \ndrier weather returns areawide late Wednesday. Low forecast \nconfidence in the evolution of a storm system late next week as a \npotent Pacific upper low moves into the Intermountain West. Ensemble \ncluster guidance is split on if the storm stays north of the desert \nSW or dives south over the desert next Friday into Saturday. A \nnorthern track will result in drier and breezy to windy conditions \nwhile a southern track will result in much wetter conditions. \nTemperatures during the long term period will be near to slightly \nbelow average across western and central NM and fairly below average \nacross eastern NM Sunday through Tuesday due to the morning low \nclouds. Below average temperatures across western NM Wednesday due \nto the morning precipitation with near average temperatures across \ncentral and eastern NM. Near average temperatures areawide Thursday.",
